                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "If there was a \"Meh\" recommendation, I'd pick it. Because at this price range, I've seen worse games, both in VR and in PC. But still, even though the graphics are nice and it has cute bears, it's not a game I'd buy if I knew what I was getting.\n\nIt's a single level game, with 60 seconds on the timer to take bears to their destination, which each delivery adding extra time. It would be a great game for relaxing and having some fun, but the short timer just ruins the experience. You don't really have time to enjoy the environment and the cute little bears jumping around (and falling to their doom when they're knocked off the platform) because you're constantly trying to get to the next point using some extremely wonky controls. \n\nAlso, even though it's advertised as a VR game, the controls are much better when playing without a headset, with a keyboard or an xbox controller (I don't think it's a coincidence that the trailer footage is from playing on your PC with a keyboard and not from the VR implementation of the game). Using the VR controllers, the forward/backwards aspect of the controls depends on the orientation of you hand, which totally ruins it in some instances if you're moving your hand around while playing like I do. The VR version ends up being a pain in the ass because the taxi easily ends up behind you and unless you're constantly turning around to find it and control it, you can't be sure of it's exact location (this doesn't happen when playing on a PC, the camera follows the taxi around). And if you do turn around to find it, you're wasting precious seconds, because then you have to turn around again as soon as it's on the right side of the playing field, which ruins your run. It shouldn't even be possible for the spaceship to go behind the player, there's no point (except if there are platforms which spawn behind you, but I don't think I'll ever get this far). The PC version isn't without fault, the camera can't be zoomed out so your field of vision is really small, which means you usually have no idea where the enemies or the objectives are. Also the game menu doesn't seem to work on PC, if you press ESC it just sits there and you can't click anything.\n\nWhat this game really needs is a Custom and/or Endless mode to make it enjoyable for the ones who play to relax and a Campaign mode to make it last for more than an hour. All the above problems wouldn't matter that much to me if I could set the timer to infinite and just enjoy the cute environment and the relaxing music. I mean, the trailer has all these cute scenes in it, but I'm never going to be able to enjoy them in-game cause I'm constantly racing the clock.\n\nThe implementation of the campaign mode would offer players a little more bang for their buck, and it could be as simple as an Angry Birds-like level system, with 1-3 stars based on your score, with scaling difficulty as you progress (less time on the clock, more enemies, smaller objective rewards, more distance between platforms). \n\nAlso, the Boss in the third mode is bugged in Oculus Quest 2, it spawns behind the player.",
